/-!
# Base change of the q-expansion function field is a function field

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.JOneES.exists_transcendental_finiteDimensional_laurentBaseChange`
(`2a4e1e30-2f75-59ed-922a-4db222b34095`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `b8e3d605-22f9-51a1-a0b2-870f8c8c1969`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $L$ be a field equipped with an algebra structure over $\mathbb{Q}$, and let $\Gamma$ be a
subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ of finite index containing the translation matrix
`ModularGroup.T`. Write $F =$ [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ Γ`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101)
for the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated
by the set `intFormRatiosC`, namely by all quotients $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\mathbb{Q}\,p_f /
\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\mathbb{Q}\,p_g$ where, for some weight $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, $f$ and $g$ are
modular forms of weight $k$ for the image of $\Gamma$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$, $p_f, p_g$ are
power series with integer coefficients satisfying the predicate `IsIntegralQExp` with respect to $f$
and to $g$ respectively, and $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\mathbb{Q}\,p_g \neq 0$. Let `laurentBaseChange L
F` be the intermediate field of $L((q))$ generated over $L$ by the image of $F$ under the
coefficientwise ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Q}((q)) \to L((q))$ induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to L$. The
assertion is that there exists an element $x$ of `laurentBaseChange L F` which is transcendental
over $L$ and such that `laurentBaseChange L F` is finite-dimensional as a vector space over the
intermediate field $L(x)$ generated over $L$ by $x$.

This is the statement that the base change to $L$ of the $q$-expansion presentation of the function
field of the modular curve attached to $\Gamma$ is an algebraic function field of one variable over
$L$, obtained from the corresponding statement over $\mathbb{Q}$. It is the hypothesis under which
the general theory of one-variable function fields (places, divisors, genus, Jacobian) is applied to
modular curves in the subsequent development, and it is invoked throughout the treatment of $X_1(M)$
and its Jacobian.

